<section id="basics">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">01</span>How the game works</h2>
  <p class="sub">If you're coming from a regular action RPG, here's the first thing to unlearn: you don't control combat here.</p>

  <p>Path of Idle is an <b>idle ARPG</b>. You don't click a skill, dodge, or aim. Your characters fight on their own, following automatic logic. What you do is <b>build the machine</b>: choose characters, skills, gear, and upgrades. Then you just watch — or close the game and come back later, since it accumulates offline rewards.</p>

  <p>That changes how much decisions weigh. In a regular ARPG, a skilled player can make up for a bad build. Here there's no such thing: <b>the build is the whole game</b>. That's why this guide spends so much time on rules that look like details — they're what separates a character that stalls at chapter 8 from one that clears chapter 20.</p>

  <h3>The core loop</h3>
  <p>You progress through the <span class="term">Campaign</span>, split into 20 chapters. Each chapter has several sites; clearing all of them unlocks the <span class="term">Boss</span>. Meanwhile, the resources you collect get invested into the <span class="term">city</span>, which is where permanent power lives.</p>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Golden rule right away:</b> every chapter's Boss <b>always</b> drops a Set piece, and treasure sites <b>always</b> drop a Legendary — but treasure sites can only be challenged <b>once</b>. Fight the Boss as soon as it unlocks; it's your reliable source of the pieces that actually matter.
  </div>

  <h3>The four activities</h3>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>Campaign</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">The main progression. Unlocks chapters, raises your level cap, and generates most of your resources and gear.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Divine Tower</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem"><b>Permanently</b> boosts Campaign resource rewards. Each floor gives Soul Shards. The Audience Chamber grants a Divinity effect that only applies on that floor.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Exploration</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">The tap you can aim: pick an island type and go after the specific resource you're missing.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Doom</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">End-game content, with its own modifiers and dedicated stages.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Prioritize the Tower early.</b> Its bonus is permanent and compounding — every hour you don't raise it is an hour everything else earns less. You can go straight for the floor's Boss, but the fight is much harder without the Divinity effect.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="teams">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">02</span>Worshiper, Godsworn &amp; teams</h2>
  <p class="sub">This is where the #1 confusion for beginners lives: the game has two different things that look the same.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Worshiper</dt><dd><b>That's you.</b> The level in the top-right corner of the screen — your account, the cult you lead. Its cap is <b>5 × the chapter number</b> (at chapter 7, cap 35).</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Godsworn</dt><dd><b>Those are the characters.</b> Each one you pull from the Prison is a Godsworn, with its own class, rarity, level, skills, and gear. They're who actually fights.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <p>Raising your <b>Worshiper</b> level does two things: it increases <b>how many Godsworn you can have</b> at once, and it improves offline rewards. The cap starts at 5 Godsworn and reaches 25 at level 100 (+1 every 5 levels).</p>

  <h3>The three teams</h3>
  <p>You don't play with a single character — you play with <b>teams of 3 Godsworn</b>, and you can have <b>three teams</b> running different adventures at the same time:</p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Team</th><th>Unlocks at</th><th class="num">Godsworn</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Team I</b></td><td>From the start</td><td class="num">3</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Team II</b></td><td>Chapter <b>4</b></td><td class="num">3</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Team III</b></td><td>Chapter <b>10</b></td><td class="num">3</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:14px">In other words: by the end you have <b>9 Godsworn deployed</b>, on three independent fronts — one team on the Campaign, another on the Tower, another on Exploration.</p>

  <h3>How to build a team of 3</h3>
  <p>There's <b>positioning</b> inside a team, and it works on two layers.</p>

  <p><b>The order of the 3 slots sets the starting formation.</b> Whoever you put in the first slot spawns in front, the second in the middle, the third in back — three fixed positions on the field. You control this by dragging Godsworn between the team's slots.</p>

  <p><b>But "the frontmost ally" is recalculated constantly.</b> During the fight, the game doesn't look at the slot number: it ranks the three by <b>each one's actual position on the field</b> at that instant. Since units move on their own — and every skill has its own cast range — whoever uses a short-range skill advances and takes the front, while long-range users fall back. The starting formation is the starting point; skill range is what holds it (or flips it).</p>

  <p>And that matters, because a lot of skills and affixes explicitly target "the frontmost ally":</p>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Transferred damage</dt><dd>Several affixes transfer part of the damage taken <b>to the frontmost ally</b> (from 12% to 43%, depending on the affix).</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Shields</dt><dd>Skills like Ice Barrier apply their shield <b>to the frontmost ally</b> specifically.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Area buffs</dt><dd>Effects like Honor Guard hit "the three frontmost allies."</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Practical composition rule:</b> put the tankiest one up front — usually the <b>Warrior</b> (Plate armor and the game's best Health growth) or the <b>Cleric</b> (also Plate). Squishier damage dealers (Mage, Priest) go in back. The classic team that works: <b>1 that tanks hits + 1 that deals damage + 1 support/auras</b>.
  </div>

  <p>And yes, <b>specialized</b> teams are absolutely worth it. Since <b>Auras</b> and "all allies" buffs only count within the team, grouping characters that reinforce each other pays off more than spreading out. Specializations that make sense:</p>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Advance team</dt><dd>Your strongest, with the best gear, pushing the Campaign to open chapters — which is what raises everyone's level cap.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Resource team</dt><dd>Doesn't need to be strong, just needs to survive. Sits on Exploration farming whatever resource is bottlenecking you.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Tower team</dt><dd>The Tower has enemies with their own patterns. A team built with the right resistances can clear floors the advance team can't.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>Watch out for the common mistake:</b> don't spread your best items across all three teams "to keep everyone average." Concentrate on the advance team — it's the one that opens chapters, and opening a chapter raises the level cap for all <b>nine</b> Godsworn. Average teams stall everyone together.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="routine">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">03</span>Daily routine</h2>
  <p class="sub">That "there's too much stuff" feeling disappears once you realize almost everything can be automated, and only five decisions actually need your attention.</p>

  <h3>Step 1 — Set it up once and forget it</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Recommended filter</dt><dd>In the auto-sell settings, turn on the <b>filter recommendations</b>. The game starts adjusting what it sells <b>by itself, based on your chapter</b>. It's the single thing that fixes inventory clutter for good.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Auto Battle</dt><dd>Unlocks after chapter one. Top-right corner of the adventure slot.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Stop on death</dt><dd>Turn it on in <b>both</b> places (challenge and Exploration). The game itself marks it as recommended — without it you get stuck in a loss loop without noticing.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Return to chapter</dt><dd>"Return to max chapter on finish" for the Tower and Exploration. Keeps the team from sitting idle.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Auto-advance</dt><dd>Auto-advance stage, auto-boss, and auto-next-chapter.</dd></div>
  </div>
  <div class="tip"><b>If you only do one thing from this section:</b> turn on the recommended sell filter.</div>

  <h3>Step 2 — The cycle of every session</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="ladder">
      <li><b>Collect offline rewards</b>There's a cap on hours — go past it and it stops accumulating. First thing to do when you open the game.</li>
      <li><b>Empty your bag</b>Open the equipment chests (hold right-click to open fast). With the filter on, junk is already sold automatically.</li>
      <li><b>Market</b>Check the <b>Orders</b>: they ask for a piece of specific quality and slot, pay in rare resources, and auto-search your bag and Warehouse on their own.</li>
      <li><b>Prison</b>Refresh looking for high rarity — but read section 07 first, there's an important rule here.</li>
      <li><b>Temple</b>Spend Blood on Blessings. Each one has a 4% chance of granting a Talent point.</li>
      <li><b>Workshop</b>Forge and Refine <b>only what's final</b>. Resources spent on a transitional piece are resources lost.</li>
      <li><b>Stele</b>Check the stage tasks — completing them early unlocks bigger upgrades on the other buildings.</li>
      <li><b>Let it run</b>Split your teams: advance on the Campaign, the others on the Tower and Exploration.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h3>Step 3 — What to keep and what to sell</h3>
  <p>This is the exact rule the game's own filter uses. With <b>T</b> as your chapter's level cap (5 × chapter):</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rarity</th><th>Sell below</th><th>Exception — always keep</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="color:#A4A3A0"><b>Common</b></td><td>level <b>T − 7</b></td><td>With <b>3 sockets</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#2B6FFF"><b>Fine</b></td><td>level <b>T − 12</b></td><td>With <b>2 sockets</b> or <b>2 Fine affixes</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#E5B426"><b>Rare</b></td><td>level <b>T − 17</b></td><td>With <b>2 Fine affixes</b> or <b>3+ Rare affixes</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#D96016"><b>Legendary</b></td><td colspan="2">Never auto-sell</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#8DC025"><b>Set</b></td><td colspan="2">Never auto-sell</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#1DC693"><b>Unique</b></td><td colspan="2">Never auto-sell</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#be212b"><b>Mythic</b></td><td colspan="2">Never auto-sell</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>The most important exception:</b> Common gear <b>with 3 sockets</b> is the <b>only</b> raw material for <b>Runewords</b> — and a Runeword is what gives your Godsworn a skill <b>from another class</b>. The official filter only flags "0, 1, and 2 sockets" for sale on purpose, sparing the 3-socket ones. If you configure the filter by hand and check "3", you'll sell off the most valuable thing a white item can be without even noticing.
  </div>

  <h3>Step 4 — Where resources come from and go</h3>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>Gold</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Campaign and Exploration → Bewitch prisoners, buy at the Market, Forge at the Workshop.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Blood of the Old Gods</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Campaign and Exploration → Bless Godsworn at the Temple, Torment prisoners.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Bone</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Stele tasks and Exploration → building Renovation, Refining at the Workshop, reshaping the Golem.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Soul Shards</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Divine Tower floors → Infuse classes.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip"><b>Awareness:</b> only goes up with <b>Rare or better</b> items (Rare gives 10, Legendary/Set/Unique 50, Mythic 200). Common and Fine give <b>zero</b> — let the filter sell those freely. What Awareness is and what it's for: <a href="#awareness" style="color:var(--t-secondary)">section 12</a>.</div>

  <h3>The five things that need your attention</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Build</dt><dd>Which set to chase and which skills go in.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Upgrade order</dt><dd>Which building to raise first with limited resources.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Refine or not</dt><dd>Legendary → Mythic <b>destroys the item</b> on failure.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Prisoners</dt><dd>When it's worth spending to raise rarity (section 07).</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>When to stop</dt><dd>If Level Suppression starts to hurt, go back and raise your cap.</dd></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="priority">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">04</span>Casting priority</h2>
  <p class="sub">The single most important mechanic in the game, and the one fewest people understand. How the AI decides which skill to use.</p>

  <p>Since you don't control combat, there's a fixed order: a Godsworn always tries the <b>highest-priority</b> skill that's ready first. The order is always this:</p>

  <div class="card">
    <ol class="ladder">
      <li><b>Support</b>Healing, shields, summoning allies and minions. <span style="color:var(--t-text-soft)">Highest priority — always fires first.</span></li>
      <li><b>Utility</b>Buffs on allies, debuffs on enemies.</li>
      <li><b>Mastery</b>The heavy-hitting skills. <span style="color:var(--t-text-soft)">This is where your damage lives.</span></li>
      <li><b>Basic</b>The "normal attack." <span style="color:var(--t-text-soft)">Lowest priority — only fires when nothing above is available.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>The consequence that breaks builds:</b> every high-priority skill you add <b>steals time</b> from your Mastery. If you fill the bar with Support and Utility, the skill that's supposed to kill things almost never fires. This is cause #1 of "my build doesn't deal damage."
  </div>

  <p>Build it like this: <b>one</b> Mastery as your main damage source, <b>one</b> Basic chosen for the <b>effect</b> it generates (Mettle, Haste, or Sunder — not for its damage), and only the Utility/Support skills whose effect really multiplies the build. If you can't explain in one sentence why a skill is there, it's probably in the way.</p>

  <div class="tip"><b>Only one Basic.</b> The three skills at the bottom of the Talent screen are fixed as Basics, and you can only have <b>one</b> active.</div>

  <h3>Martial × Spell</h3>
  <p>There's a second axis, parallel to priority: every skill is either <b>Martial</b> (combat technique) or <b>Spell</b> (casting). That matters because attributes are split by axis — <b>Martial Speed</b> and <b>Spell Speed</b> are different stats.</p>
  <div class="tip warn"><b>The game's most common waste:</b> using a "Spell Damage" item on a Martial build. It does absolutely nothing.</div>
</section>

<section id="mechanics">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">05</span>Boosts &amp; Afflictions</h2>
  <p class="sub">Two stacking systems that almost every good build leans on. Both: max 5 stacks, 4 seconds each, with duration tracked separately per stack.</p>

  <p>A "stack" is one layer of an effect. If a skill grants 1 stack of Mettle and you use it five times in a row, you have 5 — the cap. Since each one lasts 4 seconds <b>on its own</b>, keeping the cap requires applying it steadily, not all at once.</p>

  <h3>Boosts <span class="pill">bonuses on you</span></h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Mettle</dt><dd>+5% All Damage and +5% Physical Defense per stack. <span style="color:var(--t-text-soft)">At cap: +25% damage.</span></dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Haste</dt><dd>+5% Martial Speed and +5% Dodge Value per stack.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Fortitude</dt><dd>+3% Primary Attributes (Strength, Dexterity, Intelligence) and +6% Crit Resistance per stack.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Afflictions <span class="pill">penalties on the enemy</span></h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Sunder</dt><dd>−10% target's All Defense and +5% Crit Damage taken, per stack. <span style="color:var(--t-text-soft)">At cap: −50% defense.</span></dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Slow</dt><dd>−6% Movement Speed and −5% Skill Speed on the target.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Wound</dt><dd>−10% Healing received and −10% Health Regen on the target.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Shatter</dt><dd>Reduces the target's Shield effectiveness and speeds up its decay.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Sunder is close to mandatory.</b> Five stacks strip <b>half</b> the target's defense — no item in the game gives a multiplier this size this cheaply. Any damage build improves by fitting in a Sunder source. Warrior has Cruel Hook, Mage has Shockwave, and Priest has it built into their own Basic (Frost Assault).
  </div>

  <h3>Dispel &amp; Purge</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Dispel</dt><dd>Removes all Buffs from the target — <b>except Boosts</b>.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Purge</dt><dd>Removes all Debuffs — <b>except Afflictions</b>.</dd></div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">In other words: Boosts and Afflictions <b>can't be cleansed</b>. That's why stacking builds are so reliable against bosses, which usually have effect removal.</p>
</section>

<section id="states">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">06</span>States &amp; Reservation</h2>
  <p class="sub">Four states condition dozens of affixes — and there's a Reservation trick that turns one of them permanent.</p>

  <p>First things first: <b>Reservation</b> is when something (typically an Aura) locks up a fixed slice of your Health or Mana while it's active. That slice becomes unavailable — as if your bar had shrunk.</p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Default condition</th><th>What Reservation does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Healthy</b></td><td>Health above 70%</td><td>Reserving more than 30% Health, you <b>never</b> count as Healthy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Bloodied</b></td><td>Health below 30%</td><td>Reserving more than 70%, you count as Bloodied <b>at all times</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Full</b></td><td>Mana above 70%</td><td>Reserving more than 30% Mana, you never count as Full.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Depleted</b></td><td>Mana below 30%</td><td>Reserving more than 70%, you count as Depleted permanently.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>The Bloodied build trick.</b> There are lots of affixes like "while Bloodied, +X" and "Crit vs Bloodied targets." Normally taking advantage of them means walking around at low health — risky. But by reserving more than 70% of your Health you become <b>permanently</b> Bloodied <b>with a full bar</b>, turning on all those affixes with no risk. That's exactly how the <i>Bloodied Blade</i> and <i>Bloodied Slam</i> builds work.
  </div>

  <h3>Auras, Allies &amp; Minions</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Aura</dt><dd>Activates on its own in combat and gives a continuous bonus to the team. Consumes Health or Mana as a <b>reservation</b> and <b>doesn't stack</b> with another identical one.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Ally</dt><dd>A strong unit that already starts on the field, with no duration limit — but it <b>doesn't revive</b> if it dies.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Minion</dt><dd>Summoned by a skill. Individually weak, with a duration and a summon cap.</dd></div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">Allies and Minions <b>inherit</b> part of your attributes. That's why an Ally build scales with <b>your</b> Physical Attack, not theirs — gearing yourself is gearing the creature.</p>
</section>

<section id="prison">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">07</span>Getting good Godsworn</h2>
  <p class="sub">A Godsworn's rarity is the decision that compounds the most across the whole game — and it doesn't depend on luck the way it looks like it does.</p>

  <p>Every Godsworn comes from the <b>Prison</b>: you refresh the prisoner list and use <b>Bewitch</b> (costs Gold) to convert one into a Godsworn. What almost nobody notices is how much rarity actually changes:</p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rarity</th><th class="num">Talent Points</th><th class="num">Growth per level</th><th class="num">Starting attribute</th><th class="num">Legendary traits</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="color:#A4A3A0"><b>Common</b></td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">200</td><td class="num">1000</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">1×</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#2B6FFF"><b>Fine</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">250</td><td class="num">1250</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">2×</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#E5B426"><b>Rare</b></td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">300</td><td class="num">1500</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">5×</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#D96016"><b>Legendary</b></td><td class="num">4</td><td class="num">350</td><td class="num">1750</td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">12×</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#be212b"><b>Mythic</b></td><td class="num">5</td><td class="num">400</td><td class="num">2000</td><td class="num">3</td><td class="num">30×</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Read the growth column again.</b> A Mythic gains <b>double</b> the attribute per level of a Common, and has <b>5× more Talent Points</b>. With a level cap of 100, that's not "a little better" — it's a character worth two. It costs 30× more Gold, and it's still worth it: Gold comes back, 99 levels of lost growth don't.
  </div>

  <h3>Why you only ever see Common and Fine</h3>
  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>It's not bad luck.</b> By default the Prison <b>only produces Common and Fine</b> — it's hard-coded that way. Rare-or-better prisoners <b>don't appear</b> until you buy the matching upgrade in the <b>Prison's Renovation</b> tree.
  </div>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Renovation</th><th class="num">Cost</th><th>Requirement</th><th class="num">Chance once unlocked</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Rare Prisoner</b></td><td class="num">400</td><td>—</td><td class="num">~2.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Legendary Prisoner</b></td><td class="num">1,000</td><td>Rare unlocked</td><td class="num">~0.26%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Mythic Prisoner</b></td><td class="num">2,200</td><td>Legendary unlocked</td><td class="num">~0.02%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>That answers "when do Godsworn above Rare start showing up":</b> when you buy the Renovation. And looking at the odds, it's clear that <b>waiting for a Mythic to drop isn't a plan</b> — 0.02% is one in five thousand refreshes. There are Renovations that boost the higher-rarity odds by +30% to +50%, but even then Mythic stays out of reach on pure luck.</p>

  <h3>The real path: Warp</h3>
  <p>This is where the Prison gets interesting. Besides Bewitch, there are two other actions:</p>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>Torment</dt><dd>Spends <b>Blood</b> to <b>re-roll the Godsworn's primary attribute growth</b> — in other words, which attribute they'll be good at.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>Warp</dt><dd>Spends resources to try to <b>raise rarity</b> by one step. This is the real path to Legendary and Mythic.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Warp</th><th class="num">Success chance</th><th class="num">Relative cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Common → Fine</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">1×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fine → Rare</td><td class="num">40%</td><td class="num">2×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rare → Legendary</td><td class="num">20%</td><td class="num">4×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Legendary → Mythic</td><td class="num">10%</td><td class="num">8×</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:14px">In other words: you <b>manufacture</b> the good Godsworn instead of waiting for one. Grab a cheap one and Warp it up. Warp also respects the cap unlocked by the Renovation — without the Mythic Renovation purchased, no Warp goes past Legendary.</p>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>The risk:</b> Torment and Warp move the prisoner's <b>Conviction</b> (a 0–100 bar), and the extremes are final:
    <br>• <b>Conviction 0 = Breakdown</b> — the prisoner is <b>lost</b>, can no longer be operated on or Bewitched.
    <br>• <b>Conviction 100 = Frenzy</b> — locks the actions, but <b>Bewitch cost is cut in half</b>.
    <br><br>There are Renovations that give a 10% and 15% chance for Conviction to <b>not change</b> at all — those are what make this safe to do. Buy them before investing heavily in a prisoner.
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>The Frenzy trick:</b> since Frenzy halves the Bewitch cost while locking the actions, the right order is: <b>first</b> Warp and Torment until you're satisfied, <b>then</b> (if Conviction is close to 100) push it into Frenzy and recruit at half price.
  </div>

  <h3>Decide your build before recruiting</h3>
  <div class="tip new">
    <b>Preferred Skills.</b> You can mark Talent skills as <b>preferred</b>, and prisoners get a <b>higher chance of already being born with them</b>. How many you can mark depends on your <b>Stele level</b>.
  </div>
  <p>That flips the usual order: <b>first</b> pick the build, <b>mark its skills</b>, and <b>only then</b> start refreshing the Prison. Without that, you're recruiting blind and hoping.</p>
</section>

<section id="classes">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">08</span>The six classes</h2>
  <p class="sub">Every class has 3 Basic skills (pick one) and 12 tree skills. A detail that trips a lot of people up: the two weapons listed aren't alternatives — they're <b>two simultaneous slots</b>.</p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Weapon 1 + Weapon 2</th><th>Armor</th><th class="num">Health/level</th><th class="num">Mana/level</th><th>Profile</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Warrior</b></td><td>Hammer + Sword</td><td>Plate</td><td class="num">50–60</td><td class="num">40–50</td><td>Toughest — the frontliner</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Hunter</b></td><td>Bow + Dagger</td><td>Leather</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td>Dodge, beasts, and execution</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Mage</b></td><td>Staff + Tome</td><td>Cloth</td><td class="num">40–50</td><td class="num">50–60</td><td>Highest damage ceiling, squishiest</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Cleric</b></td><td>Hammer + Staff</td><td>Plate</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td>Tanky, with auras and healing</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Mercenary</b></td><td>Sword + Bow</td><td>Leather</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td>Physical, lots of procs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Priest</b></td><td>Dagger + Tome</td><td>Cloth</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td class="num">45–55</td><td>Totems and afflictions — the most technical</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>Picking your first class:</b> the <b>Cleric</b> is by far the friendliest — 6 of their 7 official builds are minimum difficulty, and they wear Plate. The <b>Priest</b> is the opposite: 5 of their 6 builds are the hardest in the game. Warrior and Mercenary sit in the middle.
  </div>

