<section id="slots">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">10</span>The 8 equipment slots</h2>
  <p class="sub">Answers the right question: no, the rarest item isn't automatically the best — and no, you don't wear all of one type.</p>

  <p>Every Godsworn has <b>8 slots</b>:</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th class="num">Qty.</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Weapon</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td>Two simultaneous weapons, one of each type for your class</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Amulet</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ring</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Helmet</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Torso</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gloves</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Boots</td><td class="num">1</td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tip"><b>Detail a lot of people miss:</b> it's <b>two weapons at once</b>, not a choice between two. That's why the Mercenary has the Double Strike mastery, which deals extra damage when both weapons are the same type.</div>

  <h3>The slot budget</h3>
  <p>Here's the answer to "does every item have a purpose?" Yes — because <b>Sets</b> require 2 or 4 pieces to activate their bonuses, and you have 8 slots to spread around. That creates a budget:</p>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>4 + 4 free</dt><dd>One full set (2- and 4-piece bonuses active) and <b>4 free slots</b> for standalone Legendary, Unique, or Mythic pieces. The most common layout.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>4 + 2 + 2</dt><dd>One full set plus the 2-piece bonus from <b>another</b> set, and 2 free.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>2 + 2 + 4 free</dt><dd>Two partial sets, taking just the 2-piece bonus from each. Useful when the 2-piece bonuses are strong and the 4-piece ones don't fit the build.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>Why "all Mythic" is a bad build.</b> Eight standalone Mythics activate <b>zero</b> Set bonuses — and Set bonuses are what make a build exist (they're what turns "I hit things" into "I hit things and that triggers something else"). A full 4-piece Set (Green) beats four loose Mythics in most builds.
  </div>

  <p>The mental rule: <b>the set is the engine, the free slots are the fuel.</b> First lock in the 4 pieces that turn on the build; then fill the rest with the best you have in the attributes the build wants.</p>

  <h3>How to compare two items</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="ladder">
      <li><b>Is it part of my set?</b>If so, it almost always wins — the bonus is worth more than raw numbers.</li>
      <li><b>Are the affixes on the build's list?</b>Every official build lists its "preferred attributes." An item with two affixes from that list beats one with a higher base number and off-list affixes.</li>
      <li><b>Is it the right axis?</b>Martial or Spell. Being on the wrong axis zeroes out the affix's value.</li>
      <li><b>Only then the base number</b>It's a tiebreaker, not the first filter.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="equip">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">11</span>Rarities &amp; upgrades</h2>
  <p class="sub">Eight rarities and four upgrade systems. The order you upgrade in matters more than it looks.</p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rarity</th><th class="num">Fine Affixes</th><th class="num">Rare Affixes</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="color:#A4A3A0"><b>Common</b></td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">—</td><td>Runeword raw material (with 3 sockets)</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#2B6FFF"><b>Fine</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">—</td><td>Transitional; up to 2 sockets</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#C13BE3"><b>Magic</b></td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">—</td><td>Refining base for Set</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#E5B426"><b>Rare</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">4</td><td>The workhorse of progression</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#8DC025"><b>Set</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">3</td><td><b>Activates the build</b> (2- and 4-piece bonuses)</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#1DC693"><b>Unique</b></td><td class="num">2</td><td class="num">3</td><td>Item's own effect</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#D96016"><b>Legendary</b></td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">9</td><td>Fills a free slot with heavy affixes</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#be212b"><b>Mythic</b></td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">9</td><td>The top — but still, a free-slot item</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>The four systems</h3>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>Forging</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Improves base attributes. The cap rises with the Workshop. A Forged piece also <b>sells for more</b>.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Socketing</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Adds affixes. Only Common (up to 3) and Fine (up to 2) roll sockets, and only above level 20.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Refining</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Common → Legendary; Magic → Set; Legendary → Mythic (and here, <b>failure destroys the item</b>).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Corruption</h4><p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem">Risky reroll, with a limited number of uses per item.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip warn">
    <b>Classic mistake:</b> a socketed item <b>can't be Refined</b>. If you plan on Refining, do it <b>before</b> socketing a rune — otherwise you lock the piece at its current rarity forever.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="awareness">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">12</span>Awareness &amp; Affix Seals</h2>
  <p class="sub">The only system in the game that lets you <b>aim</b> at what drops. Everything else is luck with a filter — the seal changes the roll itself.</p>

  <p>Every piece of equipment has an entry in the <b>Codex</b>, and each entry has its <b>own Awareness bar</b>. Here's the part that trips people up: Awareness <b>isn't a global account number</b> — it's per item. The "Iron Axe" has its own Awareness, "Azra's Crown" has its own.</p>

  <h3>How Awareness goes up</h3>
  <p>By obtaining that same piece <b>again</b>. The better the rarity of the copy that drops, the more Awareness you get:</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Rarity obtained</th><th class="num">Awareness gained</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Common · Fine · Magic</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#E5B426">Rare</td><td class="num">10</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#8DC025">Legendary · Set · Unique</td><td class="num">50</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:#be212b">Mythic</td><td class="num">200</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Once the bar fills, that item's <b>Awareness level</b> rises and the bar resets. The level cap is set by your <b>Stele level</b> — that's why the Stele shows up throughout this guide.</p>

  <h3>What it's for: sealing affixes</h3>
  <p>With enough Awareness on a piece, you can <b>seal</b> affixes on it through the Codex. A sealed affix <b>stops appearing</b> on that item — permanently.</p>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>In practice:</b> you remove the affixes that don't fit your build from the roll pool. The next copies of that item drop with a cleaner pool — better odds of getting what you actually want, without relying on pure luck.
  </div>

  <p>The cost is <b>Awareness level + Blood</b>, and it scales fast:</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="num">Seal</th><th class="num">Awareness required</th><th class="num">Blood</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="num">1st</td><td class="num">100</td><td class="num">150</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">2nd</td><td class="num">170</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">3rd</td><td class="num">260</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">4th</td><td class="num">370</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">5th</td><td class="num">500</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">6th</td><td class="num">650</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">7th</td><td class="num">820</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">8th</td><td class="num">1,010</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">50 levels total. And the game's own warning holds: <b>the more affixes you've already sealed on that piece, the more Awareness the next seal requires</b>.</p>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>The consequence that changes how you play:</b> farming the same Set piece repeatedly is <b>not a waste</b>, even after you already have one. Every copy raises that item's Awareness and gets you closer to being able to seal off its junk affixes. It's the opposite of the "I already have this one, sell it" instinct.
  </div>
</section>

