<section id="builds-how">
  <h2 class="serif"><span class="num">14</span>Use a ready-made build, or make your own?</h2>
  <p class="sub">Short answer: use the ready-made ones now, and make your own once you understand <i>why</i> they work.</p>

  <p>The game's 39 builds aren't generic suggestions — each one is built around a <b>specific Set bonus</b>, and most literally say "centered on Set gear." They exist because the game has <b>triggers</b>: a 4-piece bonus that makes skill A fire skill B. Figuring these combos out yourself, by cross-referencing 18 sets against 72 masteries, is a lot of work.</p>

  <h3>Use ready-made builds when</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>You're learning</dt><dd>They teach you the game's grammar: which skill triggers which, which attributes travel together.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>You're short on testing time</dt><dd>Testing a build here is slow — you need to collect the set pieces first. A ready-made build saves you from finding out at chapter 12 that the idea doesn't hold together.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>You want a clear target</dt><dd>The build becomes your filter: which set to hunt, which affixes to keep, which skills to mark as preferred.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Make your own when</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="kw"><dt>You've already run one</dt><dd>After taking an official build to completion, you understand the pattern and can predict what fits together.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>You found an unused trigger</dt><dd>There are 72 masteries and not all of them show up in the official builds. Combos outside the list exist.</dd></div>
    <div class="kw"><dt>You're thinking about the team</dt><dd>Official builds describe <b>one</b> Godsworn. Since you play in teams of 3, the synergy <b>between</b> the three is your territory — the game doesn't suggest anything there.</dd></div>
  </div>

  <div class="tip">
    <b>The recommended path:</b> pick an official Easy-difficulty build for your main Godsworn and take it to completion. Meanwhile, use the other two team slots to experiment. You learn at low risk, and the team keeps advancing.
  </div>

  <div class="tip new">
    <b>About having fun:</b> the difficulty rating measures <b>how much specific gear the build needs</b>, not how fun it is. A "Hard" Priest Totem build is perfectly playable — it just needs more pieces aligned to shine. If a concept appeals to you, chase it: the game is long enough for you to run a solid build <b>and</b> an experimental one at the same time, on different teams.
  </div>
</section>

